==English==

===Etymology===
From the expression {{m|en||I'd rather|t=I would rather prefer to}}, often pronounced "I druther" in some dialects.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈdɹʌðɚz/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-druthers.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|p}}

# {{lb|en|US|informal|often|_|jocular}} [[wish|Wishes]], [[preference]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1929|author=Harry McClintock|title="If I Had My Druthers"|url=https://archive.org/details/HarrymacMcclintock-IfIHadMyDruthers1929|text="If I had my '''druthers''', I would not be a king. I'd '''druther''' be just what I am than any other thing."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1950|author=Alice Walworth Graham|title=The Natchez Woman|passage="If you had your '''druthers'''—" Old Lew was always saying, "If I had my '''druthers''', gimme one of them there Shevrolics."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1957|author=Society of Arts and Crafts|title=Theatre Arts|volume=41|page=29|passage="If I had my '''druthers'''," he informs us, "I'druther be myself — not because I'm anythin' special, just because it's convenient."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1977|author=Carole Mayhall|title=From the Heart of a Woman|passage=If I'd had my "'''druthers'''," our engagement would not have been two and one half years long. But schooling made our '''druthers''' invalid.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1993|author=Les Claypool|title=DMV|passage=If I had my "'''druthers'''," I'd screw a chimpanzee. Call it, pointless.}}

====Usage notes====
* The singular form "druther" is used with the same meaning, but much more rarely.
* The noun "druthers" is sometimes used in conjunction with the verb "druther" for increased comic effect.
*: {{ux|en|If I've got my '''druthers''', I'd '''druther''' not.}}
*: or, alternatively,
*: {{ux|en|If I've got my '''druthers''', I '''druther''' not.}}
: This double formation was popularized by the American cartoonist {{w|Al Capp}} (1909-1979) in the long-running ''{{w|Li'l Abner}}'' comic strip and was also featured in the song "If I Had my Druthers" in ''{{w|Li'l Abner (musical)|Li'l Abner: The Musical}}'' (1956).

